  "transcript": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 19.24,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Have you ever noticed that it's harder to start pedaling your bicycle than it is to ride at a constant speed?"
    },
    {
      "start": 20.06,
      "end": 22.3,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Or wondered what causes your bicycle to move?"
    },
    {
      "start": 22.96,
      "end": 26.46,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Or thought about why it goes forward instead of backwards or sideways?"
    },
    {
      "start": 27.2,
      "end": 29.3,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Perhaps not, and you wouldn't be alone."
    },
    {
      "start": 30.0,
      "end": 34.9,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "It wasn't until the 17th century that Isaac Newton described the fundamental laws of motion"
    },
    {
      "start": 34.9,
      "end": 37.8,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "and we understood the answer to these three questions."
    },
    {
      "start": 37.8,
      "end": 42.34,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "What Newton recognized was that things tend to keep on doing what they are already doing."
    },
    {
      "start": 42.34,
      "end": 45.42,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "So when your bicycle is stopped, it stays stopped."
    },
    {
      "start": 45.42,
      "end": 48.44,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "And when it is going, it stays going."
    },
    {
      "start": 48.44,
      "end": 53.66,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Objects in motion tend to stay in motion, and objects at rest tend to stay at rest."
    },
    {
      "start": 53.66,
      "end": 56.26,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "That's Newton's first law."
    },
    {
      "start": 56.26,
      "end": 59.98,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Physicists call it the law of inertia, which is a fancy way of saying that moving objects"
    },
    {
      "start": 59.98,
      "end": 65.86,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "don't spontaneously speed up, slow down, or change direction. It is this inertia that you"
    },
    {
      "start": 65.86,
      "end": 70.42,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "must overcome to get your bicycle moving. Now you know that you have to overcome inertia"
    },
    {
      "start": 70.42,
      "end": 75.44,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "to get your bicycle moving, but what is it that allows you to overcome it? Well, the answer is"
    },
    {
      "start": 75.44,
      "end": 82.02,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "explained by Newton's Second Law. In mathematical terms, Newton's Second Law says that force is the"
    },
    {
      "start": 82.02,
      "end": 88.26,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "product of mass times acceleration. To cause an object to accelerate or speed up, a force must be"
    },
    {
      "start": 88.26,
      "end": 94.1,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "applied. The more force you apply, the quicker you accelerate. And the more mass your bicycle has,"
    },
    {
      "start": 94.1,
      "end": 99.3,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "and the more mass you have too, the more force you have to use to accelerate at the same rate."
    },
    {
      "start": 99.3,
      "end": 111.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "This is why it would be really difficult to pedal a 10 pound bicycle And it is this force which is applied by your legs pushing down on the pedals that allows you to overcome Newton law of inertia"
    },
    {
      "start": 111.39,
      "end": 116.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "The harder you push down on the pedals, the bigger the force, and the quicker you accelerate."
    },
    {
      "start": 116.39,
      "end": 118.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Now on to the final question."
    },
    {
      "start": 118.39,
      "end": 122.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "When you do get your bike moving, why does it go forward?"
    },
    {
      "start": 122.39,
      "end": 127.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "According to Newton's third law, for every action there is an equal and opposite reaction."
    },
    {
      "start": 127.39,
      "end": 131.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "To understand this, think about what happens when you drop a bouncy ball."
    },
    {
      "start": 131.39,
      "end": 136.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "As the bouncy ball hits the floor, it causes a downward force on the floor."
    },
    {
      "start": 136.39,
      "end": 138.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "This is the action."
    },
    {
      "start": 138.39,
      "end": 144.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "The floor reacts by pushing on the ball with the same force but in the opposite direction, upward,"
    },
    {
      "start": 144.39,
      "end": 146.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "causing it to bounce back up to you."
    },
    {
      "start": 146.39,
      "end": 151.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Together, the floor and the ball form what's called the action-reaction pair."
    },
    {
      "start": 151.39,
      "end": 154.39,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "When it comes to your bicycle, it is a little more complicated."
    },
    {
      "start": 154.39,
      "end": 160.69,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "As your bicycle wheels spin clockwise, the parts of each tire touching the ground push backwards against the earth."
    },
    {
      "start": 161.39,
      "end": 161.95,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "The actions."
    },
    {
      "start": 162.87,
      "end": 166.97,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "The ground pushes forward, with the same force, against each of your tires."
    },
    {
      "start": 167.69,
      "end": 168.31,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "The reactions."
    },
    {
      "start": 169.23,
      "end": 173.57,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Since you have two bicycle tires, each one forms an action-reaction pair with the ground."
    },
    {
      "start": 173.85,
      "end": 177.75,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "And since the earth is really, really, really big compared to your bicycle,"
    },
    {
      "start": 178.35,
      "end": 182.23,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "it barely moves from the force caused by your bicycle tires pushing backwards."
    },
    {
      "start": 182.23,
      "end": 184.73,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "But you are propelled forward."
    },
    {
      "start": 212.23,
      "end": 212.69,
      "speaker": "narrator",
      "text": "Thank you."
    }
  ],
